{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002034","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Using the PRS Model Evaluator overview framework (compare endpoint fidelity, comparable performance evidence, method family/model structure, variant coverage, covariates, and study context), both candidates are essentially siblings: identical trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported (direct match to the target EFO), same publication (UKB portability paper), same training cohort (UKB; n=391,124), same evaluation setting (selected European validation; n=19,864), and the same basic covariates (sex, age, birth date, deprivation index, 16 PCs). Performance evidence is also effectively tied: both report only partial correlation and it is nearly identical (0.0562 vs 0.0563), with no PRS-only AUC/R2 to cleanly separate them (per the performance-metrics guidance, small metric gaps and non-PRS-only metrics are weak differentiators).

Given this near-tie, the strongest remaining structural differentiator is model architecture/variant coverage: PGS002034 uses LDpred2 with 32,552 variants versus PGS001826’s penalized regression model with 45 variants. With endpoints and covariates matched, the broader LDpred2 genome-wide score is the more plausible choice to capture polygenic signal.

Heritability sanity-check: the local h2 lookup for the exact target string did not yield a meaningful match (returned unrelated vitamin traits with match_score=0), so h2 cannot be used to calibrate the observed partial-r; this limits confidence but does not change the within-list selection."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002042","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Skill-grounded comparison (prs_model_evaluator preamble + performance-metrics guidance): phenotype/endpoint fidelity is the top factor, and here both candidates are exact matches to the target (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all “Corneal dystrophy”), so neither is penalized on alignment. Transportability/study context is also essentially tied: both are from the same UKB portability paper, same training N (391,124), same evaluation ancestry mix with selected European validation, same validation N (19,321), and similarly basic covariates (age/sex/PCs etc.), which the skill notes are not heavy leakage.

With endpoint and context tied, the most comparable performance signal available is the PRS-comparable metric reported as partial correlation (partial-r). PGS002042 has higher partial-r in the selected European validation (0.0471; CI 0.033–0.0611) than PGS001835 (0.0384; CI 0.0243–0.0525), supporting slightly stronger standalone genetic signal per the performance-metrics section’s guidance to use PRS-comparable metrics when present. As an additional within-family structural tie-breaker (lower priority than endpoint/metrics per the preamble), PGS002042 uses LDpred2 with far more variants (59,944 vs 38), which is a supportive robustness signal when other fields are near-identical.

Heritability sanity-check: get_heritability_records("corneal dystrophy") returned no matching EUR h2 records, so no h2 ceiling/context can be applied; this limits confidence, but does not overturn the within-family metric/structure advantage for PGS002042."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Endpoint fidelity: All three candidates are direct matches for the target (Dupuytren contracture). This is supported by the trait-label guidance that prioritizes phenotyping_reported as the primary endpoint-fidelity field (trait_labels section): PGS001254 uses “Dupuytren's contracture” and PGS001880/PGS002092 use the near-synonymous “Contracture of palmar fascia [Dupuytren's disease]”, which is not a meaningful mismatch.

Performance evidence comparability: Per the performance_metrics guidance, explicit PRS-only metrics (e.g., “PGS AUROC (no covariates)”, “PGS R² (no covariates)”) are the most comparable cross-model signals, while full-model AUROC can be covariate-driven and is not the primary ranking axis. PGS001254 reports PRS-only AUROC=0.6646 and PRS-only R²=0.0247, plus incremental AUROC=0.0447 with only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, array), giving the cleanest, directly comparable PRS discrimination evidence in this candidate set. PGS001880 and PGS002092 (same portability-study family) report only partial correlation (partial-r ≈0.078–0.080) without PRS-only AUROC/R², making their performance evidence less directly comparable here despite larger variant counts.

Heritability sanity check: A trait-specific h2 lookup was performed (get_heritability_records for both “dupuytren contracture” and “Dupuytren's contracture”) and returned no matching EUR records, so no heritability ceiling/sanity comparison could be applied; the selection therefore relies on endpoint fidelity plus the strongest available PRS-comparable metrics."}
